Son of Yazid, son of Nuhayk. He is also said to be Shurayh ibn Hani ibn Yazid ibn al-Harith ibn Kab al-Harithi, Abu al-Miqdam. He lived during the Prophet’s صلى الله عليه وسلم lifetime but did not emigrate until after him. His father came as a delegate to the Prophet صلى الله عليه وسلم, who asked him about his eldest child. He said, “Shurayh,” and the Prophet said, “Then you are Abu Shurayh.” Before that he had been known as Abu al-Hakam. Abu Dawud, al-Nasai, and Ibn Hibban transmitted this. Muslim listed him among those who lived through both the pre-Islamic and Islamic eras. Shurayh has reports in Muslim and elsewhere from Aishah, Ali, Bilal, and others. His two sons al-Miqdam and Muhammad, al-Shabi, and others narrated from him. Ibn Sad said that he was among Ali’s supporters and mentioned with his chain that Ali sent Abu Musa to the arbitration with four hundred men under Shurayh ibn Hani, with Abd Allah ibn Abbas among them to lead their prayers. Muawiyah ibn Salih reported from Ibn Main that his father had come as a delegate and told the Prophet صلى الله عليه وسلم his own father’s name. Yaqub ibn Sufyan counted Shurayh among Ali’s commanders at the Battle of the Camel. Abu Nuaym al-Fadl ibn Dukayn said that he lived one hundred and ten years. Al-Qasim ibn Mukhaymirah said, “I have seen no one better than him.” He was killed while campaigning with Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakrah in Sijistan in the year seventy-eight. The unbelievers had seized the roads against the Muslims, and most of that army was killed. On that day Shurayh ibn Hani spoke the following verses, which show that he lived during the Prophet’s lifetime: “I have become burdened with cares and struggle against old age;
I lived for ages among the polytheists;
then I met the warning Prophet,
and after him his truthful friend and Umar;
and the day of Mihran and the day of Tustar,
and the assembly at their Siffin and the River [possibly al-Nahrawan];
and Khumayrawat and al-Mashqar—
how long, how very long, this life has been!” The name is also rendered “Yazid ibn Nuhayk.”

son of Mirdās ibn Kathīr ibn ʿAmr ibn Shaybān ibn Muḥārib ibn Fihr al-Qurashī al-Fihrī. Ibn Ḥibbān said he was a Companion and a horseman and poet. Al-Zubayrī said that his father was chief of Banū Fihr in his time and that Ḍirār was among the horsemen. No one in Quraysh was a finer poet than he, followed by Ibn al-Zibaʿrā. Ibn Saʿd said that he fought fiercely alongside the Muslims in the battles and used to say, 'I married ten of the Companions of the Prophet صلى الله عليه وسلم to the maidens of Paradise.' He is mentioned at Uḥud and the Trench. He then embraced Islam at the Conquest and was martyred at al-Yamāma. Al-Khaṭīb, however, said that he lived to take part in the conquest of al-Madāʾin and settled in Syria. Ibn Manda wrote in his notice: 'He is mentioned, but has no report'; ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb related a statement from him. Abū Nuʿaym objected that no one had counted him among the Companions or among those who embraced Islam. Ibn ʿAsākir countered that Ibn Manda was correct. Al-Dhuhlī narrated among his reports from al-Zuhrī, from al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd: 'While we were with ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf on the road to Mecca, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān said to Rabāḥ ibn al-Muʿtarif, “Sing to us.” ʿUmar told him, “If you must take up poetry, choose the verse of Ḍirār ibn al-Khaṭṭāb.”' Abū ʿUbayda said: What made known the protection granted by Umm Jamīl al-Dawsiyya, of Abū Hurayra's clan, was this: Hishām ibn al-Walīd ibn al-Mughīra killed Abū Uzayhir al-Dawsī, who was related to Abū Sufyān by marriage. His people heard of this and rushed at Ḍirār ibn al-Khaṭṭāb to kill him. He ran into Umm Jamīl's house and sought her protection. A man saw him, pursued him, and struck him; the sword's tip struck the door. Umm Jamīl stood before them and called to her people, who kept them away. When ʿUmar came to power, she thought he was Ḍirār's brother and approached him. Once he identified his lineage, he understood the story. She said, 'You are his brother only in Islam, and he is away campaigning. We know the favor you owe on his behalf.' He gave her aid as a traveler. This explicitly establishes his Islam; Abū Nuʿaym's objection has no basis. Al-Zubayr ibn Bakkār said that the woman who granted Ḍirār protection was Umm Ghaylān al-Dawsiyya. About her Ḍirār said:
'May God reward Umm Ghaylān well on my behalf, / and her women when they were disheveled and without adornment;
and ʿAwf—may God reward him well—[an unclear verb], / [another phrase concerning his joints whose sense remains unresolved].'
He said ʿAwf was her son. Al-Zubayr also cited verses which Ḍirār ibn al-Khaṭṭāb recited to the Prophet صلى الله عليه وسلم on the Day of the Conquest:
'Prophet of guidance, to you the living clans / of Quraysh have sought refuge, when refuge came too late;
when the breadth of the earth grew narrow for them / and the God of heaven became their enemy;
when the two loops of the girth met tight around the people / and they were summoned to the devastating end.
Saʿd intends to break the backs / of the people of al-Ḥajūn and al-Baṭḥāʾ.'
And further verses. He said that Ḍirār told Abū Bakr, 'We served Quraysh better than you: we admitted them to Paradise, whereas you admitted them to Hell.'

son of Ṭarīf ibn al-As ibn Thalabah ibn Sulaym ibn Fahm ibn Ghanm ibn Daws, al-Dawsi. It is also said that he was the son of Abd Amr ibn Abd Allah ibn Malik ibn Amr ibn Fahm. His byname was Dhu al-Nur. Al-Marzubani relates in his Mu'jam that he was al-Tufayl ibn Amr ibn Humamah. Al-Baghawi said, 'I think he lived in Syria.' Al-Bukhari related in his Sahih, through al-A'raj from Abu Hurayrah, who said: Al-Tufayl ibn Amr al-Dawsi came to the Messenger of God صلى الله عليه وسلم and said, 'Messenger of God, Daws has disobeyed; invoke God against them.' He said, 'O God, guide Daws.' Ibn Ishaq related in one recension of the Maghazi, through Salih ibn Kaysan from al-Tufayl ibn Amr, a lengthy account of his conversion. It says that the Prophet صلى الله عليه وسلم sent him to Dhu al-Kaffayn, the idol of Amr ibn Humamah; he burned it with fire, saying in verse:
O Dhu al-Kaffayn, I am no worshipper of yours;
Our birth is older than your birth;
I have packed fire into your heart.
It also says that, during Abu Bakr's time, he dreamed that his head was shaved, a bird came out of his mouth, a woman put him into her private part, and his son pursued him intently but could not reach him. He interpreted it to mean that his head would be cut off, the bird was his spirit, and the woman was the earth in which he would be buried; his son Amr ibn al-Tufayl would seek martyrdom without attaining it. Al-Tufayl was killed on the day of al-Yamamah, and his son lived on. Ibn Ishaq also included this account in the other recensions without a chain. Ibn Sa'd likewise transmitted it at length by another route, as did al-Umawi from Ibn al-Kalbi by another chain. Ibn Sa'd said that al-Tufayl embraced Islam in Mecca, returned to his people's country, then came to the Prophet صلى الله عليه وسلم at the lesser pilgrimage of the Fulfillment, and witnessed the conquest of Mecca. Ibn Hibban said the same. Ibn Abi Hatim said, 'He came to the Prophet صلى الله عليه وسلم with Abu Hurayrah at Khaybar, and I know of nothing transmitted from him.' I say: al-Baghawi did transmit through Isma'il ibn Ayyash, 'Abd Rabbih ibn Sulayman told me, from al-Tufayl ibn Amr al-Dawsi, who said: Ubayy ibn Ka'b taught me the Qur'an, so I gave him a bow as a gift,' and he cited the report. He called it strange. Abd Rabbih is called Ibn Zaytun and did not hear from al-Tufayl ibn Amr. Al-Tabari related through Ibn al-Kalbi that the reason al-Tufayl was named Dhu al-Nur was that when he came as a delegate to the Prophet صلى الله عليه وسلم and the Prophet prayed for his people, he said, 'Send me to them and give me a sign.' He said, 'O God, grant him light.' A light shone between his eyes. He said, 'My Lord, I fear they will say it is a deformity,' so it moved to the tip of his whip and lit his way on dark nights. Abu al-Faraj al-Isbahani also related through Ibn al-Kalbi that, when al-Tufayl came to Mecca, some Quraysh told him about the Prophet صلى الله عليه وسلم and asked him to test his condition. He came and recited some of his own poetry; the Prophet صلى الله عليه وسلم recited Surat al-Ikhlas and the two protective suras. He immediately embraced Islam and returned to his people. He related the story of the whip and its light. He said that he called his parents to Islam: his father converted and his mother did not. He called his people, and only Abu Hurayrah responded. Then he came to the Prophet صلى الله عليه وسلم and said, 'Would you like a secure fortress and protection?' meaning the land of Daws. He said: When the Prophet صلى الله عليه وسلم prayed for them, al-Tufayl said, 'I had not wanted this.' He replied, 'There are many like you among them.' He said that Jundab ibn Amr ibn Humamah ibn Awf al-Dawsi used to say in the pre-Islamic age, 'Creation has a creator, but I do not know who he is.' When he heard of the Prophet صلى الله عليه وسلم, he went out with seventy-five men of his people, and he and they embraced Islam. Abu Hurayrah said Jundab would bring them forward one by one. Amr ibn Humamah governed Daws for three hundred years, and the treaty mentioned earlier is ascribed to him. Al-Marzubani recited in his Mu'jam verses by al-Tufayl ibn Amr addressed to Quraysh, who had threatened him on his conversion:
Convey to the sons of Lu'ayy,
despite hostility and sharp anger;
That God, Lord of mankind, is one;
Exalted is His majesty above every rival;
And that Muhammad is a servant and messenger,
a guide to right direction who makes all rectitude clear;
And that God clothed him in splendor,
and raised his standing to the utmost height.
It is said that he was martyred at al-Yamamah, as Ibn Sa'd said following Ibn al-Kalbi. It is also said that he was martyred at al-Yarmuk, as Ibn Hibban said, or at Ajnadayn, as Musa ibn Uqbah from Ibn Shihab and Abu al-Aswad from Urwah said. In the biography of his son Amr ibn al-Tufayl it will appear that it was the son who was martyred at al-Yarmuk.

Another person of this name; Ibn ʿAbd al-Barr mentioned him. Ibn Abd al-Barr said that Amir ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr related from al-Tufayl ibn Malik, who said: The Prophet صلى الله عليه وسلم circumambulated, with Abu Bakr in front of him chanting the verses of Abu Ahmad ibn Jahsh, who was blind:
How lovely Mecca is as a valley;
There are my family and my children;
There I walk without a guide.

and also said to be ibn Kadādah, al-Iyadi or al-Thaqafi. Abu 'Umar said he came to the Prophet صلى الله عليه وسلم and accepted Islam, in a long report related by historians and scholars of rare vocabulary. The Messenger of Allah صلى الله عليه وسلم granted him some of his homeland. Among his words are:
'I testify by the Ancient House and by al-Safa;
a testimony accepted through His kindness.
That you are praiseworthy and blessed among us;
and in a trustworthy one, truthful in speech, sent.' Ibn Mandah said: 'Zabyan ibn Karadah. The Prophet صلى الله عليه وسلم said to him, The enjoyment of this world passes away.' 'Abd Allah ibn Harb narrated it from Yunus ibn Khabbab, from 'Ata' al-Khurasani, from him; 'Ata''s transmission from him is disconnected.

he lived into the Islamic period and took part in the conquests under Abu Bakr. Sayf ibn 'Umar narrated from him in al-Riddah: 'Khalid ibn al-Walid led us in a dawn raid against the people of Bahra', while they were unaware. A company of them were drinking at daybreak, and their cupbearer sang:
'Give me drink, both of you, before Abu Bakr's army comes;
perhaps our deaths are close, though we do not know.' I struck off his head, and his blood mingled with his wine.'
